    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** stands as our premier offensive anchor, boasting a dominant 78.42 efficiency score. Facing Mitch Bratt and his 5.90 xERA, **James Wood** gains significant value from an elite xwOBA profile and the prime leadoff spot, providing the best floor-to-ceiling combination on the board. **Austin Riley** also commands a high 74.61 rating, fueled by a favorable 16% park factor adjustment against Brandon Young, while **Brice Turang** earns a strong 71.09 start; his matchup against Ryan Feltner offers a massive 19% SP skill advantage that leverages his current high-contact form. **Corbin Carroll** remains an essential start at 65.05, and while Foster Griffin is a respectable southpaw, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s projected base is buoyed by his game-changing speed, which remains our primary weapon in the scarcity-driven SB category.\n\nOur most difficult sit-start decision involved **Josh Naylor**, who narrowly missed the final cut with a 53.40 rating. While **Josh Naylor** provides dependable production, his encounter with the formidable Nathan Eovaldi\u2014carrying an elite 3.42 xERA\u2014negatively impacted his SP skill multiplier, pushing him below the floor compared to the higher-upside matchups for **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Tyler Soderstrom**. Meanwhile, caution is warranted regarding our lineup construction as several spots are currently based on \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions. Specifically, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Max Muncy**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Isaac Paredes** are all subject to volatility if their final lineup positions shift downward, which would compress their respective daily efficiency scores and force a re-evaluation of their utility against our remaining bench options like **Seiya Suzuki** or **Mickey Moniak**.",
